  "body": "Found while working #93 (slice 2: the drawn cutter, neck and shank in the tool editor).\n\nWhat happens: a milling tool's own shape can now be drawn as a spun half-outline (`schema::ToolCutter`, set by `SetToolCutter`, `camcraft_tools::cutter_profile`). By design it is display only: the generators, the stock simulation and the reach / shank / holder laws keep reading the tool's numbers (`diameter`, `flute_len`, `shank_d`, `shank_len`). The drawn outline is held to agree with those numbers (`cutter_profile::check`: exact diameter within the flutes, flute + shank length, never wider than the stated shank above the flutes), so the numbers are always the conservative envelope - but a necked tool (a reduced neck under the flutes, the whole point of buying one) is still judged as the full shank cylinder, so reach checks over-report and refuse cuts the real tool makes.\n\nWhat should: the simulation's tool body and the reach laws may read the drawn outline where it is narrower than the numbers' cylinder, never where it would be wider - one source for the judged envelope, the same way the drawn holder feeds `tool_body` / the band stack.\n\nEvidence: `crates/app/src/tool_cutter.rs` (reader/writer), `crates/tools/src/cutter_profile.rs` (agreement law); the judged envelope lives in `tool_body` / `HolderStack` and (open) PR #837 / #843 `sim_tool_body`, band stack - this must build on those once merged, not beside them.\n\nDone means: a tool with a drawn neck narrower than its shank is judged at the neck in the reach / shank laws and drawn so by the simulation; a tool with no drawn cutter judges bit-identically to today; pinned by tests both ways.\n\nPart of #93 (follow-up, out of its scope: \"feeding the profile into the stock simulation and collision laws\").\n",
